WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.350
Film Courage: We will go back to your first
feature WILD IN BLUE.

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:06.590
You had made two short films previously, where
did you get the confidence to know that you

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.500
could write it and then execute on it.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.440
Matthew Berkowitz, Filmmaker: I just saw it
like I love confidence is really what I have.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.690
I mean you have confidence as a filmmaker
for sure but it’s more like I just had a

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:24.790
vision of what I wanted the movie to feel
like and sound like and the characters to

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:32.070
say and if I was able to articulate that then
I felt confident in my abilities I just think

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:41.510
it came through doing it for me and it’s
also like as a filmmaker you have to own what

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.130
you do right?

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:51.289
So if you own your words and you own the material
you are making and you own up to it right?

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:58.320
Then I think with that comes confidence so
you are willing to take the risks and make

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.180
the movie you want to make.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.300
It’s scary but you have to be able to have
confidence in order to do that.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.759
Film Courage: Where do you go for screenwriting
guidance?

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:08.810
Do you go to a book?

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.920
Do you go to a website?

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.560
Matthew: I’ve read Save The Cat, I’ve
read other books and I’ve read a lot of

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.560
screenplays.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.700
I think I’ve ready TAXI DRIVER screenplay
like probably 15 times, 20 times.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.639
So you just read screenplays just the same
way you watch movies to see how movies are

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:32.600
done, you read screenplays.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.850
I had a class in college where all you did
was you watched your favorite movies.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.249
You watched like [Alfred] Hitchcock’s films
and he broke down each act and each structure

00:01:43.249 --> 00:01:47.819
and you broke down the script and how it corresponds
to the movie and the movie to your script

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:50.159
and so you just keep breaking it down.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:52.649
You want to see how they shot the scene, how
they covered it.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:56.090
You watch the movie, you pause it, you look
at it, look at the shot.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.130
Why did the shot go from a dolly to a wide
and why did the wide go to the close-up here

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.329
and why is it a down angle pointed up?

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:09.179
Why do the shots exists the way they do on
screen?

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:10.179
Just look at it.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.030
That was my education, I just watched my favorite
films.

